Exceptions (no primitive prime divisor): [1, 6]
FINITE-EXACT (n<=40): every non-primitive prime of Phi_n(2), n>2, is the largest prime of n and divides
  Phi_n(2) exactly once.  (This is the classical lemma behind Bang/Zsigmondy.)
63 = 2^6-1 = Phi_1(2)Phi_2(2)Phi_3(2)Phi_6(2) = [1, 3, 7, 3]  -> Phi_6(2) = 3 = largest prime of 6, already in 2^2-1: NO primitive prime.
Catalan reading (PROVED, elementary): 2^6-1 = (2^3-1)(2^3+1) = 7 * 9 and 2^3+1 = 3^2; a primitive prime of
  2^6-1 would have to divide (2^3+1)/gcd(2^3+1,2^2-1) = 9/3 = 3 -> impossible.  The exception IS 3^2-2^3=1.
ord_9(2) = 6 <=> 9 | 63.  Powers 2^e with e<60: 2^e=2 mod 9 iff e=1 mod 6; =5 iff e=5 mod 6; =8 iff e=3 mod 6
  (FINITE-EXACT e<60; PROVED for all e since ord_9(2)=6 and 2^1=2, 2^5=32=5, 2^3=8 mod 9).
PROVED: no prime p has ord_p(2)=6 (a prime with ord_p(2)=n is exactly a primitive prime divisor of 2^n-1);
  checked hostile-style for all p<10^5.  Hence 9 is the least modulus of multiplicative order 6 for 2,
  which is why the inherited three-row exponent law lives modulo 9 with period 6, not modulo a prime.
2^n+1, n<=40, terms with no primitive prime divisor: [3]

