==============================================================================
S6. (iv) Chebyshev conjugacy, 63 = 7*9, and the typed analogy Bang(n=6) vs critical-orbit(n=3)
==============================================================================
x=y+1/y conjugates y->y^2 to x->x^2-2.  y=2 <-> x=5/2; orbit numerators under x^2-2: [5, 17, 257, 65537, 4294967297]  = Fermat numbers F_1..F_5 (pairwise coprime: every term has a primitive prime; no Bang-type exception).
Where 2^n-1 really enters x^2-2: period-n points of x^2-2 are 2cos(2 pi k/N) with N | 2^n-1 or N | 2^n+1
  (y^(2^n) = y^(+-1)).  For n=3: N | 7 or N | 9, i.e. Phi_3(x,-2) = (x^3+x^2-2x-1)(x^3-3x+1) [S4], and
  63 = 2^6-1 = (2^3-1)(2^3+1) = 7*9 is the product of the two conductors of the two 3-cycles of x^2-2.
  Bang's n=6 exception <=> 2^3+1 = 9 = 3^2 has no new prime <=> no prime-conductor 3-cycle of x^2-2 on the 2^n+1 side
  <=> no prime p with ord_p(2)=6 (S1).  THM-4139 (37): the exact 6-cycles of squaring have conductors 9,21,63 only.
  Exact-period counts of x^2-2 (all points): n=1..6 -> [2, 2, 6, 12, 30, 54]  (n=3: 6 = 3 [conductor 7] + 3 [conductor 9]).

TYPED ANALOGY.
  Source: Bang/Zsigmondy for 2^n-1, index n=6, primitive part Phi_6(2)=3 (non-primitive since 3|6, 3|2^2-1).
  Target: critical orbit of x^2+c, index n=3, primitive part F(a,b)=a^3+2a^2b+ab^2+b^3 at (a,b)=(-7,4): value 1.
  Map: NONE on objects (multiplicative group orbit vs polynomial critical orbit).  Shared predicate: 'the n-th term
  has no primitive prime divisor' <=> 'no prime p sees the base point with exact period n mod p' (both PROVED:
  p primitive for 2^n-1 <=> ord_p(2)=n; p primitive for N_n <=> 0 has exact period n under x^2+c mod p).
  Preserved structure: a 'cyclotomic-type' factorization of the n-th term over d|n: 2^n-1 = prod Phi_d(2) versus
  G_n(c) = prod_{d|n} H_d(c) (Gleason: G_1=c, G_2=c(c+1), G_3=c H_3, G_4=c(c+1)H_4, verified n<=6 in S3b), with the
  primitive part = the d=n factor.  Lost: in Bang, non-primitive primes of Phi_n(a) divide n (uniform theorem, all n);
  for H_n(a,b) the factors are pairwise coprime for n<=6 (S3b) but no uniform statement is proved here, and the
  quadratic side has infinitely many n=2 exceptions (c=-1+-1/b) where Zsigmondy has finitely many.  Sidecar at n=3: the Thue
  equation F(a,b)=+-1 = units of Z[rho] with vanishing rho-coefficient (S3).  Cheapest decisive test: Phi_6(2)=3
  (a prime dividing 6) versus F(-7,4)=1 (a unit) - computed above, exact.
  Verdict on '63 <-> -7/4': REFUTED as a structural identification.  Exact facts only: (a) f^3(0)/f(0)=2^-6=1/(63+1)
  with exponent 2^3-2=6 (equal to Bang's 6 only because 2^n-2=2n iff n=3); (b) 63=7*9 is attached to c=-2, the
  Chebyshev parameter, as the product of the conductors of its two 3-cycles (geometry note (6), THM-4139 (36)-(38)); c=-2 = -7/4 - (1/2)^2 and
  -29/16 = -7/4 - (1/4)^2 are the s=1/2 and s=1/4 points of the 3-cycle parabola centred at -7/4; (c) the parabolic
  3-cycle at -7/4 and the conductor-7 3-cycle at -2 generate the same field Q(2cos 2pi/7) (geometry note sec.2, inherited).
  Gaussian-squaring face (inherited, geometry (13)-(16), THM-3341 sec.4, THM-3333): x=2A/C of a primitive triple obeys
  x -> x^2-2 exactly, with denominators C^(2^n) and pairwise coprime odd legs, i.e. every step of such an x^2-2 orbit has
  a primitive numerator prime; the Fermat orbit of 5/2 is the |y|=2 (non-unit-circle) instance of the same conjugacy.
  SCOPE: no map found from THM-3341's Pell-hypotenuse selector to the critical-orbit exception; not claimed.

